r 2026"],[124,"14 Jan 2027"],[109,"15 Oct"],[112,"15 Oct 12:30"],[115,"16 Oct 23:59"],[103,"17 Sep"],[118,"3 Dec"],[47,"34 · 9 members"],[44,"A controller that reads a baby's heart rate and crying, decides a rocking motion, and drives a cradle. Four PYNQ Z2 boards, one communication backbone, one cradle."],[69,"Alex, Artem"],[71,"Alexandru, Val"],[6,"Are crying and heart rate linked?"],[8,"At Demo 1, on the digital twin: we look at both signals together and check whether they move together more than the model says. If they do, S3 will weigh them accordingly."],[64,"Board bring-up"],[12,"Can the timers keep up?"],[17,"COMM-2 test (Section 2.4): we measure how long each signal takes to arrive. S3 writes a timestamp on both values, so if one is late, it shows up on the LCD."],[18,"Communication"],[76,"Communication"],[79,"Communication: how the boards talk to each other"],[96,"Converts the (F, A) decision into two 1 kHz PWM signals on the motor-control connections; cradle supplies motor power."],[100,"Date"],[110,"Demo 1 (digital twin)"],[119,"Demo 2"],[27,"Design document"],[81,"Design document: the PDF"],[56,"Detail"],[91,"Detects and filters cry level from the microphone signal."],[15,"Do the two measurements arrive at the same time?"],[32,"Download the PDF"],[16,"Each sensor board cleans up its own signal first: S1 sends a ready BPM value (with trend) at 2 Hz, S2 sends the filtered cry level at 10 Hz. So S3 combines two finished values, not raw waves. The open point: the two values must arrive close enough together for S3 to combine them at 1 decision per second."],[25,"Every board has its own TX and RX on the backbone, plus a common ground. UART is point-to-point, so addressing and turn-taking are built on top of it: each board gets a 4-bit address and every frame carries a destination."],[89,"Extracts heart rate from the LED signal; conditions its own signal before sending."],[45,"Field"],[14,"First bring-up: we check both signals on the oscilloscope, before Demo 1."],[11,"First cradle time slot, before Demo 1: we measure the load (must stay above 0.8 A) and the duty (must stay under 90%)."],[20,"Full system"],[93,"Fuses the two processed values into a (frequency, amplitude) decision; timestamps both inputs so misalignment shows on the LCD."],[65,"Hardware time slots, 1 kHz PWM + comm verification"],[9,"How do we drive the motors?"],[19,"How the four boards talk to each other"],[73,"JG, Ties"],[77,"JG, Ties"],[75,"Mika, Lucca, Hugo"],[101,"Milestone"],[98,"Milestones"],[82,"Milestones: where we are in the course schedule"],[26,"One shared data wire (SDA) and one clock wire (SCL) with a common ground. S3 acts as the master, S1, S2 and S4 as slaves. Addressing is built into the protocol, so no frame header is needed."],[1,"Open assumptions"],[80,"Open assumptions: what we are still validating"],[21,"Option 1: shared UART line"],[22,"Option 2: I2C link"],[37,"Overview"],[116,"P\\&PD peer review (midterm)"],[107,"P\\&PD writing skills assignment (S1+S2 narrative)"],[62,"Per-submodule design, S1-S4"],[49,"PYNQ Z2 (Zynq-7020)"],[3,"Question"],[42,"Reading this site"],[59,"Requirements, architecture, communication protocol"],[66,"Role"],[68,"S1 Heartbeat"],[85,"S1 Heartbeat"],[70,"S2 Crying"],[86,"S2 Crying"],[72,"S3 Decision"],[87,"S3 Decision"],[88,"S4 Motor drivers"],[74,"S4 Motors"],[10,"S4 sends the two 1 kHz PWM signals (rocking speed and rocking strength) on the motor connections of the board. The cradle setup gives the motors their power."],[104,"Section 1 final: requirements & architecture, reviewed"],[113,"Section 2 final: communication protocol"],[30,"Section 2 not final"],[61,"Section 3 design description"],[122,"Section 3 final"],[36,"Sections 1 and 2"],[58,"Sections 1-2 in the design document"],[125,"Sections 4+5 + final demo"],[34,"Status"],[39,"Status"],[54,"Status"],[102,"Status"],[90,"Status: In design"],[92,"Status: In design"],[94,"Status: In design"],[97,"Status: In design"],[83,"Submodules"],[78,"Submodules: 